<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armistice Day</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Remembrance Day, Veterans Day</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Armistice Day is commemorated every year on 11 November to mark the armistice signed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at Compiègne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which took effect at 11:00 am on that day in 1918 and ended hostilities on the Western Front. The first official Armistice Day events were held in the grounds of Buckingham Palace on the morning of 11 November 1919 and included a two-minute silence for those who died in the war. Around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> many countries changed the holiday's name and several widened it to all veterans: most Commonwealth states adopted the name Remembrance Day, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> changed it in 1954. The date is a national holiday in France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-day.jpg" alt="Armistice Day" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armistice_Day,_Wall_Street.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The armistice with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was signed at Compiègne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at 5:45 am, and took effect at 11:00 am, &quot;the eleventh hour of the eleventh day of the eleventh month&quot; of 1918. According to Thomas R. Gowenlock, an intelligence officer with the U.S. First Division, shelling from both sides continued for the rest of the day and ended only at nightfall. The armistice initially ran for 36 days and had to be extended several times. A formal peace came only with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The first Armistice Day celebration began with a banquet at Buckingham Palace on the evening of 10 November 1919, hosted by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> in honour of the President of the French Republic, Raymond Poincaré. The first official events followed in the palace grounds on the morning of 11 November and included a two-minute silence for the dead of the war and those left behind. Similar ceremonies developed in other countries between the wars. </p><p>From 1939 the two-minute silence in the United Kingdom was moved to the Sunday nearest 11 November so as not to interfere with wartime production. This became Remembrance Sunday. The main service, the National Service of Remembrance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, now takes place on that day, while the two-minute silence is also observed on 11 November itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Commemorations first honoured the military dead of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and the return to peace. Around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> many countries changed the name of the day, several widened it to all veterans and a few honour civilian dead as well. Most Commonwealth states followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in calling it Remembrance Day, and in 1954 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> changed the name to All Veterans Day, later shortened to Veterans Day. The date remains a national holiday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where it is also called Jour du Souvenir, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> it is still Armistice Day.</p><p>The day has other national meanings. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> 11 November is National Independence Day, marking the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1918 after 123 years of partition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, whose forces suffered the largest casualty rate of the war, has observed it as a public holiday since 2012, and people there wear the Natalie's ramonda as a symbol. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> marks the armistice with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 4 November. On 11 November 2018, the centenary, commemorations were held worldwide, and more than 60 heads of state and government gathered at the Arc de Triomphe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armistice Day” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
